This part owns the screens feature on the server: the store, the views, the routes, the tools and
the gate. app/tools/gate.py and tests/test_gate.py are PROTECTED files that PR #51 changed by hand,
allow-list only.

Production has THREE screens already, stored before pairing existed, with no approved/paired field
(standing fact 3). The owner counts them as already approved.

Answer these, on the code, naming file and symbol:
  - PAIRING. A screen shows a six-digit code; the owner says "approve the <name> screen, code
    <digits>". Where is that code generated, how long does it live, how many guesses are allowed,
    and is the comparison constant-time? Six digits is 10^6; an unrated endpoint is brute-forceable
    in minutes. Who may call the approve path — owner only, or anyone who can reach the tailnet?
  - MIGRATION. What does this code do with the three EXISTING rows that have no approved/paired
    field — treat them as approved, as unapproved, or crash? Either wrong answer matters: treating
    unknown rows as approved is a hole, dropping them loses the owner's screens.
  - NEW-B-CAP: the owner ruled that a 21st screen is REFUSED. Is the cap actually enforced on the
    server, on every path that can create a screen, or only in one route?
  - NEW-B-LOCAL-SLIP: re-rule at this SHA.
  - THE REMOTE. New owner routes under /displays/{id}/remote/, plus screen_off and screen_remote.
    Who may drive a screen remotely? Does the remote path re-check authority per request, or trust a
    pairing done once? Can one screen's secret drive another screen?
  - Each row holds a "secret" and a "key". Does any route, view, tool result or log line put either
    where a non-owner — or a screen — can read it? That reaches the ship rule's "leak".

